  "body": {
    "id": "lem:derived-residual-scale",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every active \\(j\\in J\\), \\(\\rho\\in I\\), and \\(\\theta_j\\in[0,\\pi/2]\\), the correlation definition and \\(\\lvert\\kappa_j\\rvert\\le1\\) give \\[v_j(\\rho,\\theta_j)^2=1-\\kappa_j^2(1-\\rho)\\cos^2(\\theta_j)\\ge\\rho\\ge\\rho_0.\\] This is a lower bound for the residual variance in the limiting selected Gaussian experiment; it does not assert a finite-\\(n\\) covariance bound.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "J",
      "rho",
      "I",
      "theta",
      "kappa",
      "v",
      "rho0",
      "n"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:gaussian-experiment"
    ],
    "justification": "Compact holdout support itself prevents degeneration of the Gaussian conditional residual scale.",
    "gap": "The residual-scale margin is an algebraic consequence of the present square-root path and therefore should not be imposed as an independent positive-definiteness condition.",
    "consumer": "All Gaussian quantile, branch, and certification bounds can use the declared design constant \\(\\rho_0\\) directly.",
    "proof_tex": "Cauchy--Schwarz applied to the defining covariance ratio gives \\(\\lvert\\kappa_j\\rvert\\le1\\).  Since \\(\\rho\\in[\\rho_0,1-\\rho_0]\\) and \\(0\\le\\cos^2\\theta_j\\le1\\),\n\\[\nv_j(\\rho,\\theta_j)^2\n=1-\\kappa_j^2(1-\\rho)\\cos^2\\theta_j\n\\ge1-(1-\\rho)=\\rho\\ge\\rho_0>0.\n\\]\nThis calculation concerns only the limiting Gaussian covariance and asserts no finite-sample covariance inequality.",
